notes               = "Untitled in source. The dedication برای دخترم سایه ('for my daughter Sayeh') is printed in the outer margin. THE PUN IS THE POEM: سایه is her name and also 'shadow', so 'her shadow, from a distance, is a sunlight in our eyes' says her name while saying she is gone. بوی فراغ, the smell of parting (فراغ for فراق), and 'gone to the far side of the cage' put this with the emigration poems — see leaves-roots (193), which argues with someone who has left. Companion to moonlit-house (194), which does the same for the other daughter. 21 Farvardin = 10 April 1997. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben."
draft               = true

===persian===

خانه خلوت شد...
پر شد از بوی فراغ...
یک نفر از ما...
به آن سوی قفس رفته‌ست...
سایه‌اش... از دور...
آفتابی در نگاه ماست...
یک نفر که مثلِ باران،
در بهارِ کشتگاه ماست...
و...
به سرمایِ زمستانی که...
پیشاپیش راهِ ماست...
۱۳۷۶/۱/۲۱

===machine===

The house went quiet...
It filled with the smell of parting...
One of us...
has gone to the far side of the cage...
Her shadow... from a distance...
is a sunlight in our eyes...
One who, like the rain,
is in the spring of our sown field...
and...
against the winter cold that...
lies on the road ahead of us...
1376/1/21

===lantern===

The house went quiet.
It filled with the smell of parting.
One of us
has gone to the far side of the cage.
Her shadow, from a distance,
is a sunlight in our eyes —
one who, like the rain,
is in the spring of our sown field,
and
against the winter cold
that lies on the road ahead of us...

1376/1/21

===translation===

===footnotes===

The daughter's name is Sayeh, which is the Persian for *shadow*. So the line that says her
shadow reaches them from far off is also saying her name, and the poem can call a departure a
source of light without straining. English has to pick one of the two.
